</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">More baselines.</span>
Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Main Results ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Flash-dLLM: IO-Aware KV Caching and Parallel Decoding for Fast, Memory-Efficient Diffusion LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> compares the accuracy and decoding throughput of the evaluated cache and parallel-verification methods. Conventional baselines achieve accuracies of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="79.32\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>79.32</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">79.32\%</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="82.79\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>82.79</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">82.79\%</annotation></semantics></math> and throughputs of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14.9</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42.8</annotation></semantics></math> tokens/s. Elastic-Cache yields the highest baseline accuracy (<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="82.79\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>82.79</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">82.79\%</annotation></semantics></math>), whereas FreeDave achieves the highest baseline throughput (<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42.8</annotation></semantics></math> tokens/s).
Flash-Cache with confidence-aware decoding achieves <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="82.87\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>82.87</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">82.87\%</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="149.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>149.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">149.4</annotation></semantics></math> tokens/s, while its integration with Flash-Verify further improves performance to <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83.02\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>83.02</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83.02\%</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="210.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>210.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">210.6</annotation></semantics></math> tokens/s. These results demonstrate that combining Flash-Cache with Flash-Verify yields substantial gains in both KV-cache efficiency and parallel-decoding performance over existing baselines.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Ablation Studies and Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalability.</span>
Figure <a href="#S3.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 3.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Flash-dLLM: IO-Aware KV Caching and Parallel Decoding for Fast, Memory-Efficient Diffusion LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> reports throughput as a function of batch size on GSM8K-512 (1-shot) using LLaDA-1.5. Flash-dLLM scales nearly linearly to a batch size of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> without exhausting GPU memory, whereas Fast-dLLM encounters an out-of-memory error at batch size <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24</annotation></semantics></math>. Table <a href="#Ax1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ G More scalability analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Flash-dLLM: IO-Aware KV Caching and Parallel Decoding for Fast, Memory-Efficient Diffusion LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> extends this analysis to GSM8K-512 (5-shot), showing that all Flash-dLLM configurations (greedy, confident, verify) scale monotonically from batch sizes <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, Flash-Cache with Flash-Verify reaches <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="199.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>199.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">199.8</annotation></semantics></math> tokens/s at batch size <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S3.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 3.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Flash-dLLM: IO-Aware KV Caching and Parallel Decoding for Fast, Memory-Efficient Diffusion LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a> further shows that, at batch size <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math>, Flash-dLLM uses approximately <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="26" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>26</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">26</annotation></semantics></math> GB of GPU memory, compared with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> GB for Fast-dLLM, representing a reduction of about <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>48</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48\%</annotation></semantics></math>. This improvement arises from the flat, preallocated cache layout, which avoids dynamic memory allocation and the padding overhead associated with conventional four-dimensional KV-cache implementations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2609.26796v1/accuracy_throughput_pareto.png" id="S3.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pareto frontier of Flash-Verify</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2609.26796v1/flash_vs_confidence_pareto_paper.png" id="S3.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pareto frontiers of Flash-Verify and Confidence</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S3.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Accuracy–throughput trade-off under different track-budget values <math id="S3.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and denoising settings <math id="S3.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S3.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. The mean and standard deviation are computed over five random seeds.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy–throughput trade-off.</span>
Figure <a href="#S3.F5.sf1" title="In Figure 5 ‣ 3.3 Ablation Studies and Analysis ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Flash-dLLM: IO-Aware KV Caching and Parallel Decoding for Fast, Memory-Efficient Diffusion LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a> characterizes the accuracy–throughput trade-off across track budgets <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and Flash-Verify thresholds <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. In general, accuracy and throughput are inversely related: increasing accuracy from approximately <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>83.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> reduces throughput from <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="278" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>278</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">278</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="186" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>186</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">186</annotation></semantics></math> tokens/s. Larger track budgets generally improve accuracy at the cost of additional computation, whereas <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> provides finer control over parallelism within a fixed budget. The Pareto frontier identifies the optimal operating points across these configurations. In particular, a favorable trade-off can be obtained by maintaining a relatively large <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> while reducing <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to promote greater decoding parallelism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Flash-Verify vs. confidence-aware decoding.</span>
Figure <a href="#S3.F5.sf2" title="In Figure 5 ‣ 3.3 Ablation Studies and Analysis ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Flash-dLLM: IO-Aware KV Caching and Parallel Decoding for Fast, Memory-Efficient Diffusion LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a> compares Flash-Verify with confidence-aware decoding. Across most of their shared accuracy range, Flash-Verify delivers substantially higher throughput. At approximately <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="82.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>82.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">82.6\%</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="82.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>82.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">82.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy, it achieves <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="190" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>190</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">190</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="210" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>210</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">210</annotation></semantics></math> tokens/s, compared with <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="140" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>140</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">140</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="160" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>160</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">160</annotation></semantics></math> tokens/s for confidence-aware decoding. Although confidence-aware decoding attains a slightly higher peak accuracy of approximately <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>83.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83.4\%</annotation></semantics></math>, its throughput decreases to about <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="131" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>131</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">131</annotation></semantics></math> tokens/s. Overall, Flash-Verify provides a more favorable accuracy–throughput trade-off.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2609.26796v1/flash_conf_tpi.svg" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/217;" width="429" height="217"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S3.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Tokens-per-iteration comparison of Flash-Verify and Confidence-aware decoding.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tokens decoded per iteration.</span>
Figure <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.3 Ablation Studies and Analysis ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Flash-dLLM: IO-Aware KV Caching and Parallel Decoding for Fast, Memory-Efficient Diffusion LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> compares accuracy, throughput, and tokens decoded per iteration across Flash-Verify thresholds <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and confidence-aware thresholds <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Across denoising settings, Flash-Verify generally decodes more tokens per iteration and achieves higher throughput, demonstrating greater parallel-decoding efficiency. Because Flash-Verify incurs an additional verification step, decoding <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.2</annotation></semantics></math> tokens per iteration yields throughput comparable to confidence-aware decoding at <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.6</annotation></semantics></math> tokens per iteration; however, Flash-Verify achieves <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> higher accuracy. As the number of tokens decoded per iteration increases, the accuracy gap between the methods narrows, while Flash-Verify’s throughput advantage grows to as much as <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.33\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.33</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.33\times</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2609.26796v1/combined_accuracy_throughput.svg" id="S3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/197;" width="429" height="197"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S3.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Impact of the masked-window size <math id="S3.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> on accuracy and throughput</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Masked-window size <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.3 Ablation Studies and Analysis ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Flash-dLLM: IO-Aware KV Caching and Parallel Decoding for Fast, Memory-Efficient Diffusion LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> examines the effect of the masked-window size <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> on the accuracy and throughput of Flash-Verify and confidence-aware decoding. For both methods, accuracy improves up to an intermediate window size, whereas throughput decreases as <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> increases. Flash-Verify achieves comparable accuracy across all settings, with gaps of only <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.2</annotation></semantics></math> percentage points. Moreover, it consistently delivers higher throughput, with its speedup over confidence-aware decoding increasing from approximately <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.4\times</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\times</annotation></semantics></math> as the window size grows.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion Language Models and Acceleration.</span>
Masked diffusion models generate text by iteratively unmasking tokens predicted in parallel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Scaling this paradigm has produced models competitive with autoregressive LLMs, including LLaDA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, Dream <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, and Gemini Diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google DeepMind, 2025</a>)</cite>. However, the lack of KV caching support makes dLLM inference slow: bidirectional attention and evolving hidden states across denoising steps prevent direct reuse of autoregressive caching strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pope et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Fast-dLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> introduces prefix caching with confidence-aware decoding. dKV-Cache <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> caches at fixed temporal intervals. Elastic-Cache <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen-Tri et al., 2025</a>)</cite> makes updates adaptive via attention-pattern drift detection. FlashDLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> combines token-level change detection with an external autoregressive verifier.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallel Decoding and Speculative Verification.</span>
Speculative decoding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leviathan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite> accelerates autoregressive LLMs by drafting tokens with a fast model and verifying them in parallel with the target model. In diffusion LLMs, parallel decoding takes a different form: confidence-aware methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> unmask all tokens above a threshold per step, Prophet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> dynamically halts refinement when predictions stabilize, and FreeDave <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu and Zhang, 2025</a>)</cite> drafts tokens in one forward pass then verifies in a second. FlashDLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> uses an external autoregressive LLM as the verifier. Our Flash-Verify differs from all of these: the dLLM serves as both drafter and verifier through the KV cache, with no external model and no training. We feed both the draft predictions and the original masks at the same positions under a causal attention mask, letting the model check its own consistency in a single additional forward pass. Tokens are accepted only when the draft and mask views agree, combining the throughput of aggressive parallel decoding with the accuracy of conservative single-token unmasking.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we proposed <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Flash-dLLM</span>, a training-free inference acceleration framework for diffusion Large Language Models. Flash-dLLM addresses the key efficiency bottlenecks in dLLM decoding by jointly handling IO-aware KV caching and parallel draft-and-verify decoding. By reducing redundant KV-cache read/write operations, prioritizing influential decoded tokens, and improving the confidence of early token commitments, Flash-dLLM effectively converts the inherent parallelism of dLLMs into practical inference speedup. Extensive experiments on mathematical reasoning and code generation demonstrate that Flash-dLLM achieves superior speed, memory efficiency, and scalability over existing acceleration methods, offering a promising and practical path toward efficient deployment of diffusion-based language models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work is supported by the MBZUAI-WIS Joint Program for Artificial Intelligence Research.</p>
</div>
</section>
